as really truly tragic. The feeling that these animals are fellow citizens and should be treated with the same consideration has never fully gone away. It is not just about what we want. It's about what the squirrels and all of the other than human beings in our parks want too. And they are constituents of New York City. I go to the park every day.
确实非常悲惨。这些动物是我们的同胞，应该受到同样的对待，这种感觉从未完全消失。这不仅仅是关于我们想要什么。这是关于松鼠和我们公园里所有其他非人类生物想要什么。它们是纽约市的选民。我每天都去公园。

51
00:25:11.000 --> 00:25:42.000
People love to feed the animals in the park. On any park bench in any city, you'll see people throwing crumbs to the birds, and the birds approaching for more. It's one way we feel a connection to nature. For just a moment, both human and animal are doing something together. That shared experience between species, that's what the people at the hearing didn't want taken away. Gabriel Willow also believes that there is value in connecting with these animals, but he sees it as a reason not to feed them.
人们喜欢喂公园里的动物。在任何城市的任何公园长椅上，你都会看到人们向鸟儿投掷面包屑，而鸟儿走近索要更多。这是我们感受与自然联系的一种方式。在那一刻，人类和动物都在一起做某事。物种之间那种共享的体验，就是听证会上的人们不想被剥夺的东西。加布里埃尔·威洛也认为与这些动物建立联系是有价值的，但他认为这正是不要喂养它们的原因。

52
00:25:42.000 --> 00:26:06.000
I think people really crave a direct interaction and connection with nature. And so what I was saying when I spoke was like, look, please go to the park, observe wildlife, feel that connection. You don't need to touch them. You don't need to pet them. You don't need to hold them. You don't need to feed them. Like, it's actually more interesting and more valuable to stand back and see an
我认为人们确实渴望与自然进行直接的互动和联系。所以我发言时说的是，看，请去公园，观察野生动物，感受那种联系。你不需要触摸它们。你不需要抚摸它们。你不需要抱着它们。你不需要喂养它们。实际上，退后一步观察动物表现出更自然的

53
00:26:06.000 --> 00:26:34.000
animal exhibiting a more natural behavior. Watch a squirrel long enough and you'll see it play tricks on other squirrels. If a squirrel carrying an acorn believes it's being watched, it will dig a hole, pantomime putting the acorn in the hole, and then cover the hole back up, all while hiding the real acorn in its mouth, just to throw its fellow squirrels off the scent. Squirrels have been seen carefully organizing their acorns by size and shape before they bury them.
行为，这更有趣、更有价值。观察松鼠足够久，你就会看到它对其他松鼠耍花招。如果一只带着橡子的松鼠认为自己被监视了，它会挖一个洞，假装把橡子放进洞里，然后把洞盖上，同时把真正的橡子藏在嘴里，只是为了把它的同伴引开。人们看到松鼠在埋藏橡子之前会仔细地按大小和形状整理它们。

54
00:26:34.000 --> 00:27:04.000
Biologists believe it's a mnemonic device that helps the squirrels remember what they buried where. They have a specific call when they see a predator, so if you learn a little bit of squirrel language, like, I know the hawk call, so if I hear that I'll look up and often see a hawk. So for them to just sort of allow us to be there, but go about their day-to-day life is so much more powerful for me. There's more than one way to connect to the animals with whom we share our cities.
生物学家认为这是一种记忆辅助工具，可以帮助松鼠记住它们把什么埋在了哪里。它们在看到捕食者时有特定的叫声，所以如果你学习一点松鼠语言，比如，我知道鹰的叫声，所以如果我听到那个，我会抬头看，通常会看到一只鹰。所以让它们允许我们在那里，但继续过它们的日常生活，对我来说更有力量。与我们共享城市的动物建立联系的方式不止一种。

55
00:27:04.000 --> 00:27:34.000
Whether it's listening to the yipping of coyotes in the middle of the night, setting up a webcam for the hawk nest outside your window, or counting all the squirrels in Central Park. In March, Jamie Allen and the other team members of the Squirrel Census finished gathering their data. They plan to reveal the squirrel abundance number in June. Etienne Benson says he doesn't know how many squirrels there are in Central Park, but he thinks he does understand why everyone is so eager to find out.
无论是听深夜里土狼的叫声，为窗外的鹰巢设置网络摄像头，还是清点中央公园里的所有松鼠。三月，杰米·艾伦和松鼠普查的其他团队成员完成了数据收集。他们计划在六月公布松鼠丰度数值。艾蒂安·本森说他不知道中央公园里有多少只松鼠，但他认为他明白为什么每个人都如此渴望找出答案。

56
00:27:34.000 --> 00:28:33.000
You know, one of the appeals of a squirrel census is just the same appeal that has existed since the 1850s, which is it's clear to people that the world around them is not only inhabited by humans, that it is this multi-species place. And I think there is a need and a desire there to know something about your neighbors. The census is a form of appreciation, a way of recognizing that the squirrels are there, and that, in itself, is beautiful. Since we first broadcast this story, the 2018 Central Park Squirrel Census was released and they found 2,373 squirrels.
你知道，松鼠普查的吸引力之一与1850年代以来的吸引力相同，那就是人们清楚地认识到周围的世界不仅有人类居住，而且是一个多物种的地方。我认为人们有一种了解邻居的需求和愿望。普查是一种欣赏的形式，一种承认松鼠存在的方式，而这本身就很美。自从我们首次播出这个故事以来，2018年中央公园松鼠普查结果发布，他们发现了2373只松鼠。

57
00:28:33.000 --> 00:29:15.000
In 2020, the Squirrel Census did another flash count of 24 other New York City parks and found an additional 433 squirrels. Those numbers sit perfectly in the middle of the range I imagined in such a way that I do not know if that seems like a lot of squirrels or very few. But I'm grateful we have the number nonetheless. 99% Invisible was produced this week by Caitlin Swaljay and edited by Joe Rosenberg, mixed by Sharif Youssef, music by Swan Rial, Jenny Conley, Nate Query, and John Neufeld. Special thanks to Susan Kirby and Andrew Christopher, whose interviews did not make it into this
2020年，松鼠普查项目对纽约市其他24个公园进行了另一次快速统计，又发现了433只松鼠。这些数字恰好处于我所设想范围的中间，以至于我不知道这算是松鼠很多还是很少。但无论如何，我很感激我们得到了这个数字。本周的《99% Invisible》由Caitlin Swaljay制作，Joe Rosenberg编辑，Sharif Youssef混音，音乐由Swan Rial、Jenny Conley、Nate Query和John Neufeld创作。特别感谢Susan Kirby和Andrew Christopher，他们的采访虽然没有出现在本期节目中，但没有他们的帮助，我们就无法完成这个故事。

